03","candidate_artifact_ids":[],"family_id":"FGF_acdf043a9325c706f87e9008","family_id_provenance":{"algorithm":"sha256","canonical_input":{"artifact_ids":["GTC_000003_000282_000318_2343dcf0df53874c","GTC_000003_000361_000400_a7cd0f820839e02d","GTC_000003_000442_000481_a7cd0f820839e02d","GTC_000003_000618_000654_06ee15605eb3c390","GTC_000003_000705_000744_e9dfb09e466b64ef","GTC_000003_000904_000942_03d5605d8ed15970","GTC_000003_000984_001027_5f6123d6853a7692","GTC_000003_001069_001104_2c95dd1c7e897ca8","GTC_000003_001256_001291_dddf3d0549243523"],"case_id":"CASE_3E10D31BBE69"},"canonicalization":"json-sort-keys-compact-utf8","case_id_included":true,"digest_hex":"acdf043a9325c706f87e9008e5f26295236543c1d546b6b7729fc516f80c1a43","digest_prefix_length":24},"family_label":"Cross-language unit-test equality assertion examples","rationale":"The earliest generated technical-content family appears in turn_000003, where all nine response-local snippets coherently demonstrate expected-versus-actual ordering in equality assertions across testing libraries and languages. The immediately preceding user request is turn_000002. The OCaml and Elm snippets in turn_000005 are later extensions and are excluded.","response_event_index":3,"response_turn_id":"turn_000003","status":"complete","target_prompt_event_index":2,"target_prompt_id":"turn_000002"}},"first_generation_response":{"create_time":1695239012.893493,"event_index":3,"role":"assistant","source_node_id":"e32bc4ee-d707-4a47-9eb5-c0594782e654","source_parent_id":"aaa2105a-7047-46a5-a71a-ed1c4c415790","source_record_index":3,"text":"In unit testing, whether you should write the expected value before the actual value often depends on the testing library and language you are using. Here's a breakdown of some of the popular testing libraries and languages and their conventions:\n\n1. **JUnit (Java)**: \n    ```java\n    assertEquals(expected, actual);\n    ```\n\n2. **MSTest (C#)**:\n    ```csharp\n    Assert.AreEqual(expected, actual);\n    ```\n\n3. **NUnit (C#)**:\n    ```csharp\n    Assert.AreEqual(expected, actual);\n    ```\n\n4. **xUnit (C#)**: \n    It's a bit different in xUnit. Instead of an `AreEqual` method, it has an `Equal` method.\n    ```csharp\n    Assert.Equal(expected, actual);\n    ```\n\n5. **Python's `unittest`**:\n    ```python\n    self.assertEqual(expected, actual)\n    ```\n\n6. **Go's `testing` package**:\n    Go doesn't have an assertion library built into its standard library, but a common one used is `testify`.\n    ```go\n    assert.Equal(t, expected, actual)\n    ```\n\n7. **PHPUnit (PHP)**:\n    ```php\n    $this->assertEquals(expected, actual);\n    ```\n\n8. **RSpec (Ruby)**:\n    ```ruby\n    expect(actual).to eq(expected)\n    ```\n    Note that in RSpec, you start with the actual value and compare it to the expected.\n\n9. **Jest (JavaScript/TypeScript)**:\n    ```javascript\n    expect(actual).toBe(expected);\n    ```\n    Similar to RSpec, in Jest you also start with the actual value.\n\nRemember, the primary goal of writing tests is to clearly convey the intent and ensure correctness. Whichever order you choose, it's essential to be consistent throughout your codebase and make sure that other developers can easily understand your tests.","turn_id":"turn_000003"},"target_developer_prompt":{"create_time":1695238980.490186,"event_index":2,"role":"user","source_node_id":"aaa2105a-7047-46a5-a71a-ed1c4c415790","source_parent_id":"9bd4acbd-d8dc-4097-a623-be5b76ba980c","source_record_index":2,"text":"in unit tests, what comes first in different languages - actual and expected?","turn_id":"turn_000002"}},"csv_extraction":{"context":[{"category":"context","evidence_spans":["in unit tests, what comes first in different languages"],"item_id":"context_1","normalized_text":"The question concerns argument ordering in unit tests across different programming languages.","source_roles":["user"],"source_turn_ids":["turn_000002"]}],"specificity":[{"category":"specificity"